Welcome!

This projet's goal is to connect live time Kinect data into blender, then wrap a skin on the skeleton. In addition,
there will be goals for lighting, shading, and various properties. 

I will also provide a tutorial for installation.

PROJECT REQUIREMENTS:
 - Xbox 360 Kinect
 - Xbox 360 Kinect computer adaptor (AC - USB adaptor)
 - Developer Toolkit Browser v1.8.0 (Kinect for Windows)
 - Blender (I'm using version 4.4)

NOTES BEFORE STARTING:
 - There is a difference between an Xbox 360 Kinect and Xbox One Kinect. I am using an Xbox 360 kinect. 
 - There are two main versions of the Kinect for Windows (v1.8.0 and v2.0.0). v1.8.0 is for Xbox 360 Kinect and v2.0.0 is for Xbox One Kinect
 - There are more tutorials online for Xbox One, but this project only works with Xbox 360

STEPS:
 - Open Kinect for Windows v1.8 and install "Skeleton Basics-D2D"
 - You can reference the installation guides for any issues (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/kinect-1.8/jj131041(v=ieb.10))
 - One issue I had was that NuiApi.h does not exist. If this comes up, go to build path and include where the file is located (for me, it was at "Program Files/Microsoft SDKs/Kinect/v1.8/inc/NuiSensor.h")
 - To run this project, you need to open the SkeletonBasics.cpp file, then go to Terminal > Run Build Task
 - When building, some issues I got were a lot of converting from LPSTR to LPCWSTR. I got past this by just changing the functions to match the correct inputs
   - EX.) WNDCLASSW -> WNDCLASS
   - EX.) LoadCursorW -> LoadCursor
   - If you have the reverse issue as me, you can simply reverse my changes.
   - When I finally took care of all compiler issues, I was left with an exe file that opened an empty terminal. This can be shown by the changing the lines in CSkeletonBasics::Update() to this
```python
 if (NULL == m_pNuiSensor)
    {
        std::cout << "Program failed!" << std::endl;
        return;
    }
```
   - Which, for me, gets spammed. This tells me that the Kinect is not connected properly
 - Okay, the previous issue was due to the c program not being able to open the window in Windows, and so it failed the entire program
 - I've decided to just chop off hte visual portion and try to extract the data, which worked great! Issues all gone! Here's a snippet of my code rn
```python
for (int i = 0; i < NUI_SKELETON_COUNT; ++i)
    {
        std::cout << "Skeleton Number: " << i << std::endl;

        // Example: Print specific fields of the skeleton data
        std::cout << "Skeleton Tracking State: " << skeletonFrame.SkeletonData[i].eTrackingState << std::endl;
        std::cout << "Skeleton Position: ("
                  << skeletonFrame.SkeletonData[i].Position.x << ", "
                  << skeletonFrame.SkeletonData[i].Position.y << ", "
                  << skeletonFrame.SkeletonData[i].Position.z << ")" << std::endl;
    }
```
 - I will now be hosting the values using a localhost port. 
